💡 severity: SUGGESTION —
💡 **SUGGESTION: release harness resources on the loud-failure path too (try/finally)**

`close()`'s doc contract: cancel the output subscription ("otherwise an open stream keeps the test runner's event loop alive") and delete the harness-owned temp CWD *after* the process is dead. The new body runs `shutdownPty` **before** those calls — so when escalation fails and `shutdownPty` throws its loud `StateError`, the rest of `close()` is skipped: `_outputSub?.cancel()`, `_releaseOwnedCwd()` and `_releaseTranscript()` never run. The old semantics always released (silent give-up); the new semantics trade resource release for loudness, but you can have both:

```dart
Future<void> close() async {
  if (_closed) return;
  _closed = true;
  try {
    await shutdownPty(pty, budgetScale: waitBudgetScale);
  } finally {
    await _outputSub?.cancel();
    _releaseOwnedCwd();
    _releaseTranscript();
  }
}
```

The `StateError` still propagates and fails the test loudly; the subscription is detached and the temp CWD is cleaned up on dev machines where the process does *not* exit immediately after. Same shape applies to `hardKill()` (line 528), whose body likewise puts `shutdownPty` first.

# Thread `PRRT_kwDOTXdlLc635IMj` — 💡 SUGGESTION: release harness resources on the loud-failure path too (try/finally)

**Fixed** — commit `d3959e5a5f (branch tip; byte-identical squash 5081ad327b)` on `ai/gh-2208`; `close()` and `hardKill()` now use exactly the proposed shape.

## What changed (`test/integration/pty_harness.dart`)

```dart
Future<void> close() async {
  if (_closed) return;
  _closed = true;
  try {
    await shutdownPty(pty, budgetScale: waitBudgetScale);
  } finally {
    await _outputSub?.cancel();
    _releaseOwnedCwd();
    _releaseTranscript();
  }
}
```

`hardKill()` got the same wrap (its body likewise put `shutdownPty` first). The loud `StateError` still propagates and fails the test; the output subscription is detached and the owned temp CWD + transcript buffers are released even when escalation fails. The `close()` doc comment now states the release-on-loud-path contract.

## Regression pin (TDD)

`test/integration/pty_harness_kill_escalation_test.dart` gained `close()/hardKill() release resources on the loud path too`: it fails unless both methods wrap their `shutdownPty` call in `try { … } finally { <the three releases> }`. The terminal `StateError` path itself is not reproducible on POSIX (SIGKILL cannot be trapped), so the pin guards the structure that carries the behavior — RED against the pre-fix harness (verified: `close()` had no `try`), GREEN after.
